 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q: a sheet is admitted into a first-use queue, and promoted into a separate reused queue only once a second request hits it while it is still resident. Eviction draws from the first-use queue before touching the reused queue, so a long sequential screening — which fills the first-use queue with sheets nobody revisits — cannot push out sheets editors are actively comparing. This holds regardless of rendering concurrency or the 12 GiB bound chosen above; only the preview worker's private replacement bookkeeping changes.
+
+Two parameters are not yet fixed and need a decision before this becomes a testable spec:
+
+- **Queue structure.** Whether to use the minimal two-queue form (a FIFO first-use queue and an LRU reused queue, promoting on second access) or the original three-structure form (first-use queue, a ghost list of recently-evicted first-use keys, and the reused queue), which also resists scans when a sheet is re-requested shortly after eviction. The option sketch assumed two queues; the ghost-list variant adds a third structure.
+- **Size split between the two (or three) structures.** The fraction of the 12 GiB bound reserved for the reused queue versus the first-use queue.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q | LRU (less bookkeeping, but a long sequential screening can evict sheets an editor is actively comparing); frequency-based eviction with aging (retains repeatedly popular clips, but counter maintenance and aging lag a project switch) | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons of a small working set; 2Q isolates one-off screening sheets from reused comparison sheets so a screening can't evict them. The extra queues and a size-split parameter to tune are accepted as the cost of that protection. All three candidates were confined to the preview worker's private cache, with no change to the review API or render queue. |
